  "verdicts": {
    "robustness_carries_to_removal": "yes (conditional on image quality) + On clean images the two engines TIE on removal (33 vs 31/35, delta 2), so the clean-data thesis holds: perception is at ceiling and the decision layer is the bottleneck. Under heavy blur (r4) the advantage is LARGE and significant: removal Florence 32 vs EasyOCR 18/35 (delta 14, McNemar b=14 c=0 exact p=0.0001), tracking the detection gap (34 vs 25). Florence end-to-end removal is degradation-ROBUST across all 9 conditions (range 31-33/35); EasyOCR removal collapses under heavy blur (range 18-31/35). Mechanism: the pipeline is detection-gated, so burned-in text the weaker engine fails to READ under blur can never be redacted downstream. This refines (does not contradict) the clean-data claim and answers why a VLM: it extends the ceiling-perception regime to degraded images, where robustness carries all the way to PHI removal.",
    "prior_generalizes": "yes-via-generic-fallback (NOT via the manufacturer catalogue) + Only 4/35 GT regions match a catalogued vendor (Philips Medical Systems, partial matches), and 19/35 have an EMPTY Manufacturer tag; the remaining 31/35 are held-out (match_type default/none). Of the spatial prior's 27/35 total hits, 24 come from the GENERIC default-strip fallback (top/bottom 15%, left/right 20%) and only 3 from the proprietary manufacturer catalogue. So the prior DOES generalize to unseen/untagged vendors (held-out recall 24/31, Wilson 60.2-88.6%), but that generalization is carried by the generic margin heuristic, not vendor-specific zones. Honest qualification of the 'manufacturer spatial prior' framing: on MIDI-B burned-in data it is effectively a generic margin/strip prior with a small (3/27) manufacturer-specific component."
  },
